---
/* About page, Alistair St Pierre + Local Service Rocket agency story.
   Sourced from Found by Friday brain dumps (journey + 2026.09.09 Q1-10 agency model). */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Picture from '../components/Picture.astro';
import { absoluteUrl, breadcrumbList, graph, webPageNode } from '../data/schema';

const title = 'Meet the Agency Behind Google Growth | Local Service Rocket';
const description =
  'Meet Alistair St Pierre and Local Service Rocket: how we onboard, price, and grow home service companies on Google with fees that scale with you.';
const pageUrl = absoluteUrl('/about/');
const jsonLd = graph([
  webPageNode({ pageUrl, name: title, description }),
  {
    '@type': 'AboutPage',
    '@id': `${pageUrl}#about`,
    url: pageUrl,
    name: title,
    description,
    mainEntity: { '@id': 'https://localservicerocket.com/#business' },
  },
  breadcrumbList(pageUrl, [
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'About', path: '/about' },
  ]),
]);
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <main>
    <section class="hero container">
      <div class="crumbs">
        <a href="/">Home</a>
        <span>/</span>
        <span class="current">About</span>
      </div>
      <div class="eyebrow">ABOUT</div>
      <h1>Google systems for owners building something bigger than a truck.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Local Service Rocket is run by Alistair St Pierre from Wellington, New Zealand. The work is
        simple to say and hard to do well:
        <a href="/services/websites">websites</a>,
        <a href="/services/local-seo">SEO</a>, and
        <a href="/services/paid-ads">advertising</a>
        that turn Google into booked jobs, then scale with you when the company can handle it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="container split">
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">WHO I AM</div>
        <h2>A systems thinker who still touches the work.</h2>
        <p>
          I’ve always been technical, building on computers since I was young, studying media
          design, teaching technical concepts, and working in product and games. What I’m good at
          isn’t jargon. It’s seeing a system (ads, Google Business Profile, website, sales process)
          and figuring out what has to be true for it to produce customers.
        </p>
        <p>
          I run the agency like a master tradesperson runs a job: contractors handle filler work
          (design direction, builds, setup tasks) under a clear brief. I set the standard, check the
          start and end of every important piece, and make sure what ships can actually beat the
          competition on Google, because ranking is a contest, not a checklist.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="aside-card">
        <div class="aside-person">
          <Picture
            name="alistair-photo"
            alt="Alistair St Pierre, founder of Local Service Rocket"
            class="aside-photo"
            width={96}
            height={96}
          />
          <div>
            <div class="aside-name">Alistair St Pierre</div>
            <div class="aside-role">Founder · Wellington, NZ</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="aside-label">QUICK FACTS</div>
        <ul>
          <li>Based in Wellington, New Zealand</li>
          <li>Serves home service companies in the US (and ANZ)</li>
          <li>Starter engagements from about $500/month per service lane</li>
          <li>Prove results before we ask you to spend big</li>
          <li>Focus: Google presence, paid ads, converting websites, hiring systems</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container story">
      <div class="measure">
        <div class="measure__spine" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <div class="measure__copy">
          <div class="eyebrow">A NORMAL WEEK</div>
          <h2>Reports in. Trends watched. Problems fixed fast.</h2>
          <p>
            Day to day, I’m watching whether Google is moving the needle: booked calls from leads that
            come through the website, Google Business Profile, LSAs, and ads. Perfect channel-by-channel
            attribution is messy. What matters is overall booked work climbing from the Google side, and
            jumping on it when it isn’t.
          </p>
          <p>
            Owners (or someone in their office) typically send a simple pulse: how many calls booked
            today. I track the trend. When setup is solid and the market is calm, the system can run
            quietly. When something slips, that’s where my time goes. I’m not pinging you constantly for
            meetings; I’m accountable for outcomes.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="measure__rail" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container story">
      <div class="measure">
        <div class="measure__spine" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <div class="measure__copy">
          <div class="eyebrow">HOW THE AGENCY STARTED</div>
          <h2>From “I don’t understand marketing” to running it for trade owners.</h2>
          <p>
            Before Local Service Rocket, I was the technical / operations person inside someone else’s
            growing business, with no equity and no real marketing skill. Earlier projects I’d started
            never took off for the same reason. So I left, poured income into learning ads properly, and
            discovered how unforgiving the stack is: if targeting, tracking, offer, phone process,
            landing page, or Google Business Profile is wrong, nothing prints money.
          </p>
          <p>
            My first paid client was <strong>$500 USD / month</strong>. I didn’t build a sales machine
            first, I built delivery until it worked, then took the next client. Same model years later:
            start small, prove it, grow with the owner.
          </p>
          <p>
            That path has included helping companies add serious Google-driven revenue, think owners in
            the high-six-figures pushing into multi-million run rates, and launching brands like
            <strong>Dry Duck</strong> from nothing to heavy monthly revenue in months, with the owner in
            the trenches beside me. It’s always a partnership.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="measure__rail" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="dark-band">
      <div class="container dark-inner">
        <div class="eyebrow on-dark">HOW WE WORK DIFFERENTLY</div>
        <h2>Most agencies optimize for closing. We optimize for staying.</h2>
        <div class="points">
          <div class="point">
            <div class="num">01</div>
            <div>
              <div class="point-title">Prove it before you pay big</div>
              <p>
                Starter pricing exists so we can show movement before five-figure retainers. The
                industry burned trust chasing setup fees. We earn the next dollar of fee when you’re
                ready to scale.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="point">
            <div class="num">02</div>
            <div>
              <div class="point-title">Clear expectations, no hype</div>
              <p>
                Marketing isn’t guaranteed. What I can promise is a process that’s worked, honest
                timelines and costs, limited early budgets, and relentless problem-solving when
                something breaks.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="point">
            <div class="num">03</div>
            <div>
              <div class="point-title">I lead the Google playbook</div>
              <p>
                I abandoned the habit of deferring to an owner’s marketing hunches. If we disagree on
                the fundamentals, we’re not a fit, and that’s healthier than a slow failure.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="point">
            <div class="num">04</div>
            <div>
              <div class="point-title">Master the basics, win the market</div>
              <p>
                Fancy “secret strategies” are usually noise. There’s still a massive gap of companies
                that don’t do the basics of Google extremely well. That’s the game we play.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container story">
      <div class="measure">
        <div class="measure__spine" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <div class="measure__copy">
          <div class="eyebrow">WORKING TOGETHER</div>
          <h2>What onboarding actually looks like.</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>First call.</strong> I’m listening for commitment, not vibes. One practical signal:
            are you willing to generate reviews and do the owner-side work Google requires? It’s a
            two-way partnership. I bring leads and systems; you sell, service, and show up as a real
            business on Google. We do a fast audit, find the biggest Google bottleneck, and decide what
            to start with. New companies often begin on a small starter lane (~$500 setup / month style
            pricing). Larger companies may hand over multiple lanes at once, and we’ll talk honestly
            about the limits of splitting SEO and ads across agencies.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>First 7 days.</strong> Setup work is scheduled and usually largely complete for the
            kickoff scope.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>First 30 days.</strong> We start reading outcomes from that setup. Sometimes you see
            signal in days; sometimes you need closer to two months, especially newer brands. That’s
            another reason starter pricing stays sane: I don’t want you funding a long quiet period at
            agency-premium rates.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="measure__rail" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container story">
      <div class="measure">
        <div class="measure__spine" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <div class="measure__copy">
          <div class="eyebrow">PRICING PHILOSOPHY</div>
          <h2>Fees step up when your company steps up.</h2>
          <p>
            I like making more when you make more. Typical path: start with a starter SEO or ads lane
            around <strong>$500/month</strong> each, often introduced separately, get organic working,
            then add ad management when it makes sense. When you’re ready to spend meaningfully more on
            advertising, my fee increases with that scale.
          </p>
          <p>
            Why? At $50k/month the risk is different than at $200k/month with trucks, techs, and payroll
            built around demand. Keeping Google performing at that level is where the risk lives, and
            where experienced problem-solving is worth paying for. SEO still matters for foundation and
            maintenance; beyond a point, dollars are usually better spent competing in ads than
            over-investing in SEO retainers.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What I won’t sell:</strong> outcome guarantees. I tried that once with heavy upfront
            money. It created the wrong relationship. The scaling model already rewards delivery, I
            don’t need a guarantee gimmick on top.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="measure__rail" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container split soft">
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">THE HARD CONVERSATIONS</div>
        <h2>Leads are my job. Selling them is yours.</h2>
        <p>
          My side is high-quality lead flow from Google. Your side is converting those leads:
          estimating, upselling the right work, staffing, and operations. When ads start booking and
          ticket size or close rate is soft, the fix is often sales process, not “bad leads.” I’ll
          say that clearly. The clients who last are the ones who can hear it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">AFTER 12 MONTHS</div>
        <h2>How I hope you’d describe me.</h2>
        <p>
          Competent. Trustworthy. Fair. Easy to talk to. Fast. If that’s how it feels to work
          together, we’re doing it right.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container story">
      <div class="eyebrow">NEW ZEALAND TIME ZONES</div>
      <h2>Oddly perfect for trade owners.</h2>
      <p>
        Relative to the US I’m usually about 3-6 hours shifted depending on coast and daylight
        saving. Most marketing conversations happen at the <em>end</em> of an owner’s day, which is
        morning or midday here. You can call after the last job, on the drive home, or from the couch.
        You’re not fighting for a 9am slot with an east-coast agency that’s already offline.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="container story">
      <div class="eyebrow">FRAMEWORK + JUDGMENT</div>
      <h2>Productized enough to be repeatable. Custom enough to win.</h2>
      <p>
        Everything I deliver starts from a framework, what belongs on a site, how a profile should
        look, how an initial ad account should be built. What goes <em>into</em> that framework
        changes per market: competition, keyword difficulty, brand age, ticket size. If a head term
        is a brick wall, we may earn authority on a related angle first, then transfer strength
        later. Same playbook, different field conditions.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="container story">
      <div class="eyebrow">WHY AI SHOWS UP IN OUR WORK</div>
      <h2>Same standards. Faster build. Cleaner SEO discipline.</h2>
      <p>
        When AI tooling got good enough, I stress-tested it building <strong>Dry Duck</strong>:
        brand + site + Google systems from a tiny brief. Same conversion and SEO principles, shipped
        dramatically faster than WordPress-and-plugin workflows. The point isn’t magic. It’s encoding
        what already works so we can iterate without breaking the basics.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="container cta-block">
      <h2>If you want a partner who treats your leads like their reputation</h2>
      <p>
        Watch how it works and what it costs, then book onboarding. Your first call is about your
        business, not a pitch deck.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a href="/get-started" class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">Schedule onboarding</a>
        <a href="/case-studies" class="btn btn-secondary btn-lg">See client results</a>
      </div>
      <p class="fine">
        Or call <a href="tel:8883716365">(888) 371-6365</a> · Wellington, NZ · Serving US home
        service companies
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding-top: 64px;
  }

  .crumbs {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 10px;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--muted-3);
    margin-bottom: 22px;
  }

  .crumbs a {
    color: var(--muted-3);
  }

  .current {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  h1 {
    margin-top: 14px;
    font-size: clamp(30px, 4.5vw, 46px);
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    line-height: 1.1;
    text-wrap: balance;
    max-width: 18ch;
  }

  .lede {
    margin-top: 16px;
    font-size: 17.5px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--muted);
    max-width: 52ch;
  }

  .split {
    margin-top: 64px;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1.4fr 1fr;
    gap: 40px;
    align-items: start;
  }

  .split.soft {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
  }

  .split h2,
  .story h2 {
    margin-top: 10px;
    font-size: clamp(24px, 3vw, 30px);
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .split p,
  .story p {
    margin-top: 14px;
    font-size: 15.5px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--body);
  }

  .aside-card {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-panel);
    padding: 24px;
    background: var(--section-bg);
  }

  .aside-person {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 14px;
    padding-bottom: 18px;
    margin-bottom: 18px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }

  /* :global() because <Picture> renders the <img> from a child component, so it
     never carries this page's scope attribute. */
  .aside-person :global(.aside-photo) {
    width: 68px;
    height: 68px;
    flex: none;
    border-radius: 99px;
    object-fit: cover;
    display: block;
  }

  .aside-name {
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  .aside-role {
    margin-top: 2px;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .aside-label {
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--muted-3);
  }

  .aside-card ul {
    margin: 14px 0 0;
    padding-left: 18px;
  }

  .aside-card li {
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--body);
  }

  .aside-card li + li {
    margin-top: 10px;
  }

  .story {
    margin-top: 64px;
  }

  .dark-band {
    margin-top: 72px;
    background: var(--dark);
    color: var(--dark-text);
    padding: 64px 0;
  }

  .eyebrow.on-dark {
    color: var(--orange-on-dark);
  }

  .dark-inner h2 {
    margin-top: 10px;
    font-size: clamp(24px, 3vw, 32px);
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    max-width: 640px;
  }

  .points {
    margin-top: 32px;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 18px;
  }

  .point {
    display: flex;
    gap: 14px;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    padding: 20px;
  }

  .num {
    font-weight: 800;
    color: var(--orange-on-dark);
    font-size: 14px;
    flex: none;
  }

  .point-title {
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 15.5px;
  }

  .point p {
    margin-top: 6px;
    font-size: 13.5px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--dark-muted);
  }

  .cta-block {
    margin-top: 72px;
    margin-bottom: 72px;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .cta-block h2 {
    font-size: clamp(24px, 3vw, 30px);
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .cta-block > p {
    margin-top: 12px;
    font-size: 15.5px;
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .cta-row {
    display: flex;
    gap: 12px;
    justify-content: center;
    margin-top: 22px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  .fine {
    margin-top: 18px !important;
    font-size: 13px !important;
    color: var(--muted-2) !important;
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .split,
    .split.soft,
    .points {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
